"In Japan, convenience stores (konbini) are on every corner, open 24/7. Make solutions accessible and people will use them."
"Germany reunified in 1990 and is now Europe's strongest economy. Recovery from division is possible with commitment and time."
"In Finland, libraries loan out everything from books to musical instruments and even sports equipment. Share resources — everyone benefits."
"Australia has a town called '1770' named after the year Captain Cook landed. Your journey creates markers for those who follow."
"In the UAE, Friday-Saturday is the weekend. Convention isn't universal — create your own structure."
"Canada has the longest coastline in the world (202,080 km). Your opportunities are limitless — you just need to explore."
"In Switzerland, every male must serve in the military or pay extra taxes. Contribution comes in many forms — find yours."
"Sweden created IKEA, Spotify, and Volvo. Small countries can have global impact — size doesn't limit ambition."
"The Netherlands has the world's first stock exchange (1602). Innovation often means creating what doesn't exist yet."
"In Singapore, 80% live in government housing, yet it's world-class. Public solutions can be excellent with the right execution."
"Norway's Government Pension Fund invests oil money for future generations. Think beyond today — build for tomorrow."
"In New Zealand, sheep outnumber people 5:1, yet tech is booming. Don't be defined by stereotypes — write your own story."
"Japan has 'inemuri' — napping at work is seen as a sign you're working hard. Different cultures solve problems differently — stay open."
"In the UK, tea time isn't just a break — it's a ritual. Small daily practices create culture and connection."
"Germany has 'Feierabend' — a word for the end of the work day that means celebration time. How you finish matters as much as how you start."
"In Finland, saunas outnumber cars. Prioritize what actually improves well-being, not just what looks impressive."
